  "summary": "The Bible describes different kinds of sacrifice with different purposes. The cited passages include Noah's burnt offering, the covenant rite at Sinai, the offerings in Leviticus, and Hebrews' account of Christ's self-offering; reading each scene closely matters more than assuming every sacrifice does the same thing.",
  "historical_context": "The cited passages place sacrifice in several textual settings: Noah's offering after the flood, Abraham's test, Israel's covenant ceremony, the burnt offering and Day of Atonement instructions in Leviticus, and Hebrews' account of Christ's offering. These examples describe the biblical texts and do not establish a comparison with neighboring religious practices.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "",
  "hebraic_worldview": "",
  "second_temple_context": "",
  "canonical_context": "The passages distinguish several roles. Genesis 8 narrates Noah's burnt offering; Genesis 22 tells of a ram offered in place of Isaac; Exodus 24 associates blood with the covenant; Leviticus 1 gives instructions for a burnt offering, and Leviticus 16 describes the Day of Atonement. Psalm 40 emphasizes doing God's will, Isaiah 53 portrays a suffering servant, and Hebrews 9 interprets Jesus' offering in relation to cleansing and access to God. These passages connect, but their images and purposes should not be collapsed into one ritual formula.",
  "later_christian_reception": "",
