  "literary_context": "Notice the form of each passage: Genesis 8 and 22 tell narratives, Exodus 24 recounts a covenant ceremony, Leviticus 1 and 16 give ritual instructions, Psalm 40 is a poem, Isaiah 53 is a prophetic song, and Hebrews 9 develops an argument about priesthood and sacrifice. Psalm 40's emphasis on obedience and Hebrews' use of sacrificial language show that the canon interprets these practices in more than one way.",
  "covenantal_significance": "Exodus 24 connects blood with the covenant made with Israel; Leviticus 16 describes rites of purification on the Day of Atonement; and Hebrews 9 presents Christ's self-offering as cleansing the conscience for service to God. These passages relate sacrifice to covenant, cleansing, and access, while the distinct rites and arguments explain why a single summary cannot capture them all.",
